WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
Tiers inside one family.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:11.843
Each major provider sells a ladder of models from the same family: a large, most capable tier; a balanced middle tier; and a small, fast, cheap tier.

3
00:00:12.123 --> 00:00:15.643
Anthropic’s ladder at time of writing is Claude Opus 5.

4
00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:25.224
5 (its documentation’s suggested starting point for most workloads, aimed at complex agentic coding and enterprise work), Claude Sonnet 5.

5
00:00:25.504 --> 00:00:32.501
5 (speed and capability for everyday coding, agent, and enterprise workloads), and Claude Haiku 5.

6
00:00:32.781 --> 00:00:38.051
5 (lowest latency and price, for real-time and high-volume work and sub-tasks).

7
00:00:38.331 --> 00:00:40.357
Above them sits Claude Fable 5.

8
00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:47.080
2, described as Anthropic’s most capable widely available model, for the hardest long-horizon work.

9
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.240
OpenAI and Google publish similar ladders.

10
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.339
Anthropic’s model-selection guide describes two ways to start.

11
00:00:54.619 --> 00:01:03.621
Capability-first: build with the strongest model, get the prompt right, then try cheaper settings or smaller tiers and keep them where quality holds.

12
00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:08.701
Efficiency-first: start with the small tier and move up only where tests show a gap.

13
00:01:08.981 --> 00:01:14.699
The first is better for hard, high-stakes tasks; the second for high-volume, well-defined ones.

14
00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:27.160
The same guide makes a point that is easy to miss: many current models have an effort setting that trades thoroughness for speed and cost within one model, and tuning it is often a better lever than switching models.

15
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:35.568
Whichever path you take, the guide’s decisive step is the same one this lesson ends on: build tests from your actual prompts and data.
